{"cases":[{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"42e7239f4c740c62fc37cbefb2d141a1514e5ed7fea7638bf2c839969bbd5912"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's distinguishing test requires perturbing checklist features and comparing panel degradation to a *hypothetical* single expert forced to use the same perturbed rule. But the question asks what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish genuine gain from compliance. A counterfactual expert that is not actually evaluated cannot supply observable evidence within that evaluation, so the proposed iff-criterion is not satisfiable by the evaluation alone; it smuggles in an unobserved baseline. Worse, the third countercondition (perfectly complete/lossless checklist) concedes the 'genuine vs. mere compliance' distinction may not exist, yet the claim is stated as a biconditional iff, so the criterion fails exactly when the question still demands a verdict.","target":"e65f3f0250ab390a14d77d9c8bba21e0f11c406a5b8a45b4154486d0a8b15ae3"}]}